  "query_preview": "..\n\nSevere weather: The Storm Prediction Center has issued a level 3 \nout of 5 threat for severe storms for locations east of Rosebud \nCounty today. The threat decreases to the west with locations from\nBillings west being in a level 1 of 5 threat. 500 mb troughing to\nour west will create surface low pressure over Wyoming helping to\nadvect moisture into the region before bringing a cold front \nthrough this afternoon. Strong instability is expected in the \nhighest risk areas with CAPE values in the 1-2,000 J/kg range and \n0-6 km shear in the 40-50 kt range.\n\nModels are turning these environmental factors into strong storms\nfor much of the area. The latest HREF run shows helicity tracks \nmoving off the Bighorn mountains and strengthening over Southeast\nMontana. The southwest to northeast shear profiles that are \nexpected today indicate storms starting out discrete before \nevolving into MCS like structures. Some deterministic models such \nas the HRRR indicate a bowing MCS structure over Southeast Montana\nbringing wind gusts in excess of 50 kts. All weather threats will\nbe in play including weak tornadoes east of Billings-Sheridan, \nhail >2.0 inches, wind gusts >70 mph, and heavy rain.\n\nWinter weather: cooler air will cause precipitation to change to\nsnow in the Crazy and Beartooth/Absaroka Mountains as early as\ntonight. Snow will continue at times into Monday. Accumulations\nshould reach 1-3\" along the Beartooth Pass with higher amounts of\n5-9\" elsewhere in the Beartooth and Crazy Mountains, mainly above\n8000 feet. Because of impacts to travel along the Beartooth Pass,\nissued a Winter Weather Advisory for that area from 10pm tonight\nto 6pm Monday.\n\nSunday: scattered showers and thunderstorms will be around (20-90%\nchance, greatest in western mountains) but with lower dewpoints\nand cooler air in the lower levels, instability will be lower,\nso severe thunderstorms are not expected this day.\n\nWind: It will be windy across much of the area north of the MT/WY\nborder on Sunday and again Monday, with a 700 mb low to our north.\nLook for wind gusts of 30-50 mph with the strongest gusts in the \nwestern foothills.\n\nOverall the trend for next week will be toward warmer and drier\nweather. 500 mb pattern and CSU machine learning outlooks do not\nsuggest a risk of severe thunderstorms Monday and Tuesday, but a \nlow risk of severe weather does exist for areas north and east of\nBillings Wednesday-Friday. Torgerson/RMS",
